# Every tracked text file is LF in the index and in the worktree, on every
# platform. `eol=lf` is a repository rule, so it wins over a contributor's
# `core.autocrlf` and the OS that materialises the checkout cannot change it.
#
# This is not a style preference. A CRLF `#!/bin/sh\r` makes the kernel look for
# an interpreter literally named "/bin/sh\r", producing the Docker startup error
# "exec /usr/local/bin/entrypoint.sh: no such file or directory" (issues #150, #77).
* text=auto eol=lf

# Redundant under the rule above, kept deliberately: these are the files where a
# CRLF regression is a broken container rather than a noisy diff. `scripts/outis`
# is extensionless, so it is named rather than matched.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf
entrypoint.sh text eol=lf
docker/entrypoint.sh text eol=lf
scripts/outis text eol=lf

# Windows-native scripts are LF too. Verified on this estate: cmd.exe parses
# LF-only batch including `goto` into labels and multi-line `if errorlevel (...)`
# blocks, and both Windows PowerShell 5.1 and PowerShell 7 parse LF-only .ps1.

# Binary assets — never normalize.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.webp binary
*.pdf  binary
*.ico  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
